Building Dials from the source repository
==========================================

These notes are for building Dials from a checkout of its source
repository.  To build from a release tarball, see README and INSTALL.

A checkout does not contain the generated files that a release
tarball has, so building it needs these tools as well:

  Git
  GNU Autoconf    2.71 or later
  GNU Automake    1.16 or later
  GNU gettext     0.21 or later (for autopoint)
  GNU Texinfo     (for makeinfo)
  help2man        (for the manual page)
  pkg-config

Dials uses Gnulib, the GNU portability library, which the repository
includes as a Git submodule.  Clone with:

  git clone --recurse-submodules <repository>

or, in a clone made without that option, run:

  git submodule update --init

Then run:

  ./bootstrap
  ./configure --enable-gcc-warnings
  make
  make check

The bootstrap script copies the Gnulib modules listed in bootstrap.conf
into lib/ and m4/.  To use a newer Gnulib, update the submodule and run
./bootstrap again.

The --enable-gcc-warnings option turns on many compiler warnings and
treats them as errors.  Please use it while changing the code.

'make syntax-check' checks the sources in the repository for common
mistakes and for the style that GNU programs follow.  It looks at
committed files, so commit before running it.  Settings for it are in
cfg.mk.

Some tests read tests/root, a small made-up copy of /proc and /sys
whose values are known, through the --sysroot option.  When code
starts reading a new file, add it there and to tests/sysroot.sh.

'make distcheck' builds a release tarball and checks that it builds,
installs and passes its tests.  The tarball's ChangeLog is made from
the Git history.  Checking the printed manual also needs
texi2dvi from GNU Texinfo and a TeX installation.

Please follow the GNU Coding Standards
<https://www.gnu.org/prep/standards/> in new code.

